**Tinnitus** is when you hear a sound, such as **ringing, buzzing, humming, hissing or whooshing**, that does not come from outside your body. It can be in one or both ears, or in your head. Around **1 in 7 adults** in the UK have it.

## Common causes

- **hearing loss**, including age-related hearing loss
- exposure to **loud noise**
- an **ear infection** or a build-up of **earwax**
- **Ménière's disease**
- some medicines
- stress and anxiety, which can make tinnitus more noticeable

Sometimes no cause is found.

## When to see a GP

See a GP if tinnitus does not go away, is getting worse, or is affecting your sleep, concentration or mood. Your GP may look in your ears and refer you for a **hearing test**.

{% callout type="warn" title="Get help quickly" %}
Call **NHS 111** or see a GP urgently if you have tinnitus with:

- **sudden hearing loss** in one or both ears
- tinnitus that **beats in time with your pulse** (pulsatile tinnitus)
- dizziness, or weakness or numbness in your face

Go to **A&E** if you have tinnitus after a head injury.
{% /callout %}

Read about [tinnitus treatment](/conditions/brain-nerves-and-senses/tinnitus/treatment/).
